@page "/report"
@inject AppData Data

<PageTitle>Technical report · PaleCheck</PageTitle>

@if (_m is null || _model is null)
{
    <p class="muted">Loading…</p>
    return;
}

@{
    var a = _m.Audit;
    var v3 = _m.ProtocolHistory[^1];
    var leak = _m.LeakageDemo;
    int lowCut = (int)Math.Round(_model.Thresholds.Low * 100);
    var op = _m.OperatingCurve[lowCut];
    var phone = Screening.PhoneFirst(0.591, op[1], op[2]);
    int n = _m.Dataset.Anaemic + _m.Dataset.Healthy;
}

<article class="report">
    <p class="no-print small"><a href="palecheck-report.pdf" download>⬇ Download as PDF</a> · <a href="science">Interactive version</a></p>
    <header>
        <p class="report-kicker">Technical report · EurekaDev 2026 · palecheck.zetalabs.in</p>
        <h1>Copies, light and honest accuracy: auditing a public eyelid-anaemia dataset and building a calibrated phone check</h1>
        <p class="report-meta">PaleCheck project · code and data: github.com/d3osaju/palecheck</p>
    </header>

    <section class="abstract">
        <h2>Abstract</h2>
        <p>
            Phone apps promise to detect anaemia from a photo of the inner eyelid, and the largest public dataset for the task,
            CP-AnemiC, reports up to AUC 0.835. We audited its @a.Images images. <b>@a.Unreliable (@((a.ShareUnreliable * 100).ToString("0"))%)</b>
            are copies of a photograph that also appears under another patient record: @a.ImagesInDuplicateGroups pixel-identical,
            @a.NearAdded re-encoded and @a.RecropAdded re-cropped or mirrored. The @a.Images records contain only <b>@a.DistinctPhotographs
            distinct photographs</b>. Under random cross-validation a 1-nearest-neighbour "memoriser" scores AUC @leak[0].Memoriser.ToString("0.00")
            on the published data and @leak[^1].Memoriser.ToString("0.00") once all copies are removed, while a simple colour model stays near
            @leak[^1].ColourModel.ToString("0.00"). On the @n independent images, nested cross-validation gives AUC
            @_m.NestedCvAll.AucMean.ToString("0.00") (95% CI @_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooledCi95[0].ToString("0.00")–@_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooledCi95[1].ToString("0.00")).
            A physically modelled warm bulb shifts eyelid redness @_m.Lighting.Illuminants[0].TimesDiseaseGap.ToString("0.0")× more than anaemia does;
            a printed white card removes the shift. We release PaleCheck, an offline, multilingual phone app built around these limits;
            the audit tools DupeScope and the Benchmark Lab; and cleaned dataset manifests.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>1 Introduction</h2>
        <p>
            Anaemia affects 59.1% of Indian girls aged 15–19 [2]. Clinicians look for pallor of the palpebral conjunctiva (inner
            lower eyelid), and several studies train image models on conjunctiva photographs [1, 5]. Reported accuracies are high,
            but small medical datasets are prone to <i>leakage</i>, where test images are not independent of training images, a
            well-documented cause of inflated results across machine-learning-based science [7]. We set out to build a screening
            app and audited the data first.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>2 Data and audit</h2>
        <p>
            CP-AnemiC contains @a.Images cropped conjunctiva images of children aged 6–59 months from 10 Ghanaian hospitals, each linked to
            a laboratory haemoglobin value; anaemia is Hb &lt; 11 g/dL [1, 6]. We searched for photographs reused across records in three passes:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li><b>Identical:</b> MD5 of decoded RGBA pixels. @a.ImagesInDuplicateGroups images in @a.DuplicateGroups groups (up to @a.LargestGroup copies;
                Hb within one group spans up to @a.MaxHbSpread.ToString("0.0") g/dL).</li>
            <li><b>Near-identical:</b> @a.NearRule. @a.NearAdded more images.</li>
            <li><b>Re-cropped:</b> @a.RecropRule; mirrored orientation also tested. @a.RecropAdded more images in @a.RecropClusters clusters
                (@a.MirroredPairs mirror-image pairs).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            As a control for pass 3 we re-scored the same candidate pairs with one image mirrored, which preserves colour and texture
            statistics but destroys a true pixel match. At the threshold the control matched only genuine mirror-image copies
            (difference 0); the next-closest control pair lay above the threshold. Every copy cluster lists different patients
            (hospital, age, sex or haemoglobin), so no record in a cluster can be verified; all @a.Unreliable images were excluded.
        </p>
        <table>
            <caption>Table 1. Copies of a photograph filed under more than one record.</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Pass</th><th>Images added</th><th>Cumulative excluded</th><th>Images kept</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Identical</td><td>@a.ImagesInDuplicateGroups</td><td>@a.ImagesInDuplicateGroups</td><td>@(a.Images - a.ImagesInDuplicateGroups)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>+ Near-identical</td><td>@a.NearAdded</td><td>@(a.ImagesInDuplicateGroups + a.NearAdded)</td><td>@(a.Images - a.ImagesInDuplicateGroups - a.NearAdded)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>+ Re-cropped / mirrored</td><td>@a.RecropAdded</td><td>@a.Unreliable</td><td>@a.ReliableKept</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>3 Methods</h2>
        <p>
            <b>Features.</b> Tissue pixels (outline mask, glare and shadow removed) are converted sRGB → linear → XYZ → CIELAB (D65).
            Seventeen colour features are computed: absolute colour (mean and 25th-percentile a*, L*, b*, erythema index, chromaticity,
            saturation, a* spread) and within-image contrasts (percentile differences of a* and erythema index, and others).
            <b>Model.</b> L2-regularised logistic regression fitted by Newton's method, λ by inner 5-fold CV [8].
            <b>Protocol (v3).</b> After exclusion, 8 demo images are held out; the rest are split 70/30 into development and a lockbox,
            stratified by label. The feature set (absolute, relative or all) and λ are chosen by nested CV on development only; the
            lockbox is evaluated once. We also report nested 5×5-fold CV with 5 repeats over all @n images, leave-one-hospital-out,
            subgroups and bootstrap 95% CIs (2,000 resamples). <b>Leakage demonstration.</b> At each cleaning level, a memoriser
            (1-nearest-neighbour on standardised absolute features) and the colour model (λ = 1) are compared under random 5-fold CV.
            <b>Lighting.</b> Eyelids are re-lit with blackbody illuminants (Planckian-locus fit [4]) by von Kries scaling in linear sRGB, and
            corrected by dividing by a white card photographed under the same light. <b>Software.</b> NumPy pipeline; a C# re-implementation
            in the app is unit-tested to reproduce the Python features to 10⁻⁹; CI re-downloads the dataset and re-derives every number.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>4 Results</h2>
        <h3>4.1 Copies inflate benchmark scores</h3>
        <table>
            <caption>Table 2. AUC under random 5-fold CV (5 repeats). The memoriser knows nothing about anaemia.</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Images</th><th>Memoriser</th><th>Colour model</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            @foreach (var r in leak)
            {
                <tr><td>@r.Level</td><td>@r.Images</td><td>@r.Memoriser.ToString("0.00")</td><td>@r.ColourModel.ToString("0.00")</td></tr>
            }
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            On the published data, @((a.LeakedShare * 100).ToString("0"))% of test images have a pixel-identical twin in the training folds
            (identical-copy layer alone), and a twin-copying rule reaches @((a.MemoriserAccuracy * 100).ToString("0.0"))% accuracy. The memoriser's
            advantage persists after removing identical and near-identical copies and disappears only when re-crops are removed, while
            the colour model is stable across all levels, consistent with copies, not colour, driving the memoriser.
        </p>

        <h3>4.2 Honest accuracy</h3>
        <table>
            <caption>Table 3. Results by protocol version. Each version re-ran the full protocol with a new random lockbox.</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>Images kept</th><th>Nested-CV AUC</th><th>Lockbox AUC (95% CI)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            @foreach (var v in _m.ProtocolHistory)
            {
                <tr><td>v@(v.Version): @v.Rule</td><td>@v.ReliableImages</td><td>@v.NestedCvAuc.ToString("0.00")</td>
                    <td>@v.LockboxAuc.ToString("0.00") (@v.LockboxAucCi95[0].ToString("0.00")–@v.LockboxAucCi95[1].ToString("0.00"), n = @v.LockboxN)</td></tr>
            }
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            Nested CV is stable at about @_m.NestedCvAll.AucMean.ToString("0.00") across versions (v3 pooled 95% CI
            @_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooledCi95[0].ToString("0.00")–@_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooledCi95[1].ToString("0.00")); the v3 lockbox
            (@v3.LockboxAuc.ToString("0.00"), n = @v3.LockboxN) is too small to narrow this. Leave-one-hospital-out AUC is
            @_m.LeaveOneHospitalOut.PooledAuc.ToString("0.00"). Development data selected the "@_m.ChosenFeatureSet" feature set
            (@string.Join(", ", _m.FeatureSetSelection.Select(kv => $"{kv.Key} {kv.Value:0.00}"))). Estimating the haemoglobin value
            itself did not beat predicting the mean (MAE @_m.HbRegression.Mae.ToString("0.00") vs @_m.HbRegression.BaselineMae.ToString("0.00") g/dL).
        </p>
        <div class="report-figs">
            <RocChart Points="_m.Roc" Auc="_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooled" Label="(nested CV)" Operating="_m.OperatingPoints.Values" />
            <DistributionChart Healthy="_model.Reference.HealthyHistogram" Anaemic="_model.Reference.AnaemicHistogram"
                               HealthyLabel="Healthy (Hb ≥ 11)" AnaemicLabel="Anaemic (Hb < 11)" />
        </div>
        <p class="caption">Figure 1. Left: ROC curve (nested CV), with the app's two cut-offs. Right: out-of-fold pallor index of healthy and anaemic reference eyes.</p>

        <h3>4.3 Lighting</h3>
        <table>
            <caption>Table 4. Mean shift in eyelid redness (a*) when re-lit, relative to the healthy-vs-anaemic gap of @_m.Lighting.DiseaseGap.ToString("0.0").</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Light</th><th>a* shift</th><th>× anaemia gap</th><th>After card correction</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            @foreach (var il in _m.Lighting.Illuminants)
            {
                <tr><td>@il.Kelvin K</td><td>+@il.ShiftUncorrected.ToString("0.0")</td><td>@il.TimesDiseaseGap.ToString("0.0")×</td><td>@Math.Abs(il.ShiftWithCard).ToString("0.0")</td></tr>
            }
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            In simulation the card correction is exact by construction; a real camera adds auto white balance and tone curves. The app's
            Lighting Lab measures the residual on a real phone (protocol in the repository).
        </p>

        <h3>4.4 Should a photo decide who is tested?</h3>
        <p>
            At the app's cautious cut-off (pallor index ≥ @lowCut; sensitivity @((op[1] * 100).ToString("0"))%, specificity @((op[2] * 100).ToString("0"))%)
            and 59.1% prevalence, photo-first screening of 1,000 girls needs @phone.BloodTests blood tests instead of 1,000 but misses
            @phone.Missed of @phone.Anaemic anaemic girls. Where anaemia is this common, a photo should not replace testing; PaleCheck
            is positioned as a prompt to get tested for people outside school screening.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>5 The PaleCheck app</h2>
        <p>
            A Blazor WebAssembly progressive web app (English, Malayalam, Hindi) that runs offline; photos never leave the device. It
            detects a printed white calibration card automatically, selects the eyelid with one tap (region growing in CIELAB),
            rejects glare and shadow, corrects for the room light, averages up to five photos and reports a pallor index with an explicit
            overlap zone, never a diagnosis. Every result links to the free Anaemia Mukt Bharat haemoglobin test [3]; a symptom checklist
            recommends testing regardless of the photo. Two research tools ship with it: DupeScope (in-browser duplicate and re-encoding
            detection for any image dataset) and the Benchmark Lab (Table 2, reproducible live).
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>6 Limitations</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Only @(n + _m.Dataset.DemoHoldout) independent images remain; all estimates have wide intervals.</li>
            <li>The re-crop search compares each image with its @RecropNeighbours nearest colour neighbours, so some copies may remain.</li>
            <li>Reference eyes are young children in Ghana photographed without a colour reference; Indian teenagers need a new,
                calibrated dataset and the 12 g/dL threshold [6].</li>
            <li>Lighting results are simulated; real-camera validation is pending. Not a medical device.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>7 Ethics, data and AI use</h2>
        <p>
            No new human data were collected. CP-AnemiC is used under CC BY 4.0; we publish only derived manifests and colour features.
            The app stores numbers, never photos, and only on the user's device. Code and text were developed with the help of an AI
            coding assistant; every number in this report is generated by the published scripts and re-checked by continuous integration.
            We intend to share the audit with the dataset's authors.
        </p>
    </section>

